          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Point-free topology
            refers to various formulations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            that are not based on the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/points"} points
            equipped with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
          %p
            First, it conveniently describes the points not only in one’s favourite
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck toposes
          %p
            Second, it opens up the possibility of using predicate geometric theories, to obtain generalized spaces (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+toposes"} classifying toposes
          %p
            The phrase “point-free topology” is often taken as synonymous with
            %em pointless topology
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Johnstone83"} Johnstone 83
          %h2#examples_and_definitions Examples and Definitions
          %h3#locale_theory Locale theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale+theory"} locale theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subspaces"} open subspaces
            (with the extra structure of a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the frame, then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
            is that of completely prime filters of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_a
            for each element of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi a
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_a \vdash \phi_b
                (if
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq b
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊤
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \vdash \phi_1
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi a
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi b
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_a \wedge \phi_b \vdash \phi_{a\wedge b}
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                            %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mi i
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi a
                            %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{\bigvee_i a_i} \vdash \bigvee_i \phi_{a_i}
          %p
            This simply makes a direct correspondence between the algebra of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and the geometric logic. Of course it also corresponds to the finite intersections and arbitrary unions of open sets, which shows how geometric logic is matched to topology.
          %p
            The classifying topos is the topos of sheaves over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p The frame is a presentation-independent representation of the theory. It can be recovered from the theory as the Lindenbaum algebra of formulae modulo equivalence.
          %h3#propositional_geometric_theories Propositional geometric theories
          %p
            A presentation of a frame by generators and relations corresponds more directly to a general propositional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
          %h3#formal_topology Formal topology
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+topology"} formal topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+for+a+topology"} basic
            open subspaces, with a
            %em cover relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ◃
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\triangleleft U
            between elements and subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ◃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangleleft
            may be the full cover relation or a generating part, and there may sometime be included the extra structure of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positivity+predicate"} positivity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posite"} posite
            and so gives rise to a geometric theory of flat continuous functors. Its propositional symbols are
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_a
            for each
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in B
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊤
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi B
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \vdash \bigvee_{a\in B} \phi_a
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
