              %em formal points
          %h3#general_definition General definition
          %p
            It is probably impossible to give a real definition of “a notion of point-free topology”. However, one way to capture the notion of “point-free-ness” is by a version of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+category"} well-pointedness
          %p
            Specifically, suppose that one defines a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            (or
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            one thinks of as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(X,Y)
            to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            (or
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} groupoid
            %em all
            continuous maps from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            (whereas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob(S)
            need not be the class of all spaces). Also suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi pt
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            %strong pointwise
            formulation of topology if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generator"} generator
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %strong pointless
            if this is not provable (or, for a stronger variation, provably false).
          %h2#pointwise_reasoning Pointwise reasoning
          %p
            The traditional way of doing topology using points may be called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong pointwise topology
          %p
            It is possible to circumvent this by reasoning in an entirely
            %em pointless
            style, without reference to the points. For instance with locales, the maps are defined to be the frame homomorphisms, backwards.
          %p
            Surprisingly, however, it is also possible to reason pointwise: a map from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            can be defined by saying how points of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            transform to points of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in the topos of sheaves over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em geometric
            \- preserved by inverse image functors. Thus continuity becomes a logical issue, of geometricity.
          %p
            Further details and references are in Vickers
            %a{:href => "#Vickers07"} 2007
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Vickers14"} 2014
          %h2#bundles Bundles
          %p
            A decisive advantage of point-free topology is that it gives a fibrewise topology of
            = succeed "," do
              %strong bundles
            %em bounded
            geometric morphisms.)
          %p
            It is shown in
            %a{:href => "#JoyalTierney84"} Joyal, Tierney
            that, for any elementary topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            (i.e. localic geometric morphisms into it) and internal
            %em point-free spaces
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
          %p
            Joyal and Tierney proved their result for internal frames. However, in some ways it works better for presentations of frames by generators and relations, if there is some geometric theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}_0
            of which the systems of generators and relations are models. This is not the case for frames - the frame structure is not geometric.
            %a{:href => "#Vickers07"} Vickers 2007
            works it out for the general case of “GRD-systems”, systems of generators, relations and “disjuncts”. Each model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            of the geometric theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}_0
            itself presents another geometric theory (for its point-free space), and this can be used to make a theory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}_1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝕋
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon \mathcal{S}[\mathbb{T}_1] \to \mathcal{S}[\mathbb{T}_0]
            between the classifying toposes. Then a presentation in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            is a map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\colon\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{S}[\mathbb{T}_0]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p
            What this account reveals is that the bundle is - using
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            \- a continuous space-valued map on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(x)
            \- because fibres are just pullbacks.
          %h2#set_of_points Set of points
          %p Depending on the foundational setting, a point-free space may or may not have a set of points, a discrete coreflection. This can be done in topos theory, but relies on an impredicative use of power_sets_. (Formal topology arose out of a desire to work predicatively.)
          %p However, even when it exists, the set of points does not generally represent the space well.
          %p
            This is best understood in terms of bundles over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon Y\to X
            is a sheaf over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q\colon Z\to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %em opfibrational
            property that limits their ability to approximate general bundles. Suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊑
                  %mi x
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \sqsubseteq x'
            are two points of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that are related by the specialization order. Then there is a corresponding fibre map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q^{-1}(x) \to q^{-1}(x')
          %p
            As an example, take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to be the Sierpinski space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕊
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\ast\}
            and include
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo =
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ⊑
                    %mo ⊤
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot=\emptyset\sqsubseteq\top=\{\ast\}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Z
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Z
                      %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_{\bot}\to Z_{\top}
          %p
            Now consider the bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝕊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\colon 1\to\mathbb{S}
            that picks out the closed point
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
            are singleton and empty. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Z
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝕊
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q\colon Z \to \mathbb{S}
            be its discrete coreflection. Because there is a bundle map from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ⊤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_\top
            must be empty. But then because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            is a local homeomorphism,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_\bot
            must also be empty.
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕊
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
          %p We see that a non-trivial space can have have an empty set of points for purely topological reasons, nothing to do with logic or a pathological nature of point-free spaces.
          %h2#applications Applications
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach-Tarski+paradox"} Banach-Tarski paradox
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+homotopy+theory"} localic homotopy theory
          %h2#references References
          %p An introduction:
          %ul
            %li#Johnstone83
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              \:
              = succeed ";" do
                %em The point of pointless topology
              %strong 8
              1 (1983) 41-53 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/bull/1983-08-01/S0273-0979-1983-15080-2/home.html"} ams:1983-08-01/S0273-0979-1983-15080-2
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.bams/1183550014"} euclid:bams/1183550014
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (advertised as the “trailer” for
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "#Johnstone82"} Johnstone 1982
          %p Monographs:
          %ul
            %li#Johnstone82
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+Spaces"} Stone Spaces
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 3
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/de/academic/subjects/mathematics/logic-categories-and-sets/stone-spaces?format=PB&isbn=9780521337793"} ISBN:9780521337793
            %li#PicadoPultr12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jorge+Picado"}> Jorge Picado
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ales%CC%8C+Pultr"} Aleš Pultr
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frames+and+Locales"} Frames and Locales
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-0348-0154-6"} doi:10.1007/978-3-0348-0154-6
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            treatment:
